content.text:
The plan is big for two reasons. One is a goal bigger than "do research in a regimented way". The other is a pattern the project's own records name as a trap.

**1. The goal set on 2026-11-12 is not simple research.** The plan quotes your objective: a pipeline that "can be run in all directions at any stage" to "dynamically research, adjudicate, synthesize, specify and render across a multitude of languages and jurisdictions". It adds "efficient and centralized and clean", no hard-coded behaviours, and no dead ends. That asks for a machine with:
- stages that can re-enter each other;
- a comparison key of parameter × group of disabled people;
- a typed record of why a step went backward;
- a render stage;
- vocabularies that change by data, not by code.

The plan's eight "what stands in the way" items are gaps against that goal. Most of the task files exist to close one of them.

**2. The plan grew through a loop the project already documented.** DR-2026-08-19 §2.1 says "meta-work is self-catalysing by construction". Each audit produced tasks, each task produced decisions, each decision (OD-01 to OD-72) produced adjudications, and each adjudication produced more tasks. The batch notes show it: 129 tasks, with design files, register pages, gap filings and critic passes around them. Each step was reasonable by itself. Nothing in the loop asks whether the book got better, so it has no natural stopping point. [ASSUMPTION: this is my reading of the documents, not a measured causal history.]

**Only part of it is necessary.** Of the eight items, two protect the core directly: guards that a wrong figure or misquote passes, and nowhere to hold a translation. The rest serve the in-all-directions goal. If you drop that goal for now and keep "research with verifiable citations and translation", most of the 129 tasks are unneeded. Keeping the goal means accepting something close to the current size. That decision is yours, and the Opus adjudication I commissioned should address it. I'll report its findings when it finishes.
